.Dd November 5, 2026
.Dt CLOCK 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm clock
.Nd ask the authenticated operator session's time owner to inspect or correct time
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Cm status
.Nm
.Cm set Ar UNIX_SECONDS
.Nm
.Cm sync
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is an operator shell builtin, handled over the session's private control
channel by sshd, the existing NIC/time owner. Ordinary command children do not
inherit its clock-write grant or session-control ends.
Authenticated SSH exec requests such as
.Dq clock status
or
.Dq clock sync
also run through the session shell and its control channel; no PTY is required.
The exec request text is passed to the shell unchanged.
.Pp
.Cm status
shows the successful source, known/stale state, age, generation and last
synchronization attempt or error. The same read-only information is available
as
.Nm date Fl -status .
.Pp
.Cm set
steps software civil time to decimal Unix seconds, in the supported 1970..2099
range. It is an operator assertion with unknown absolute UTC uncertainty, not
an authenticated time claim. Forward and backward steps are allowed without a
maximum-step bound on this explicit assertion. The RTC bootstrap floor does
not restrict an operator assertion. Pending
network synchronization is canceled, and the next periodic attempt waits its
configured interval. The RTC and monotonic deadlines do not change.
The assertion is not sticky: a later successful periodic estimate replaces it
when the difference is within
.Cm max-step-seconds .
A larger difference leaves the assertion in place and records a maximum-step
failure, error -34, at each attempted interval until the operator changes the
assertion or disables the provider. This error does not establish a network fault.
Set and sync requests are logged with their authenticated SSH connection or enrolled local console
and shell process identity where available.
.Pp
.Cm sync
requests one bounded configured SNTP attempt. The reply means scheduled, not
successful: inspect status for the actual outcome. It refuses without a
configured provider or NIC owner. An unavailable carrier records a failure
once and waits for carrier; automatic timeout retries back off to the normal
interval and peer refusals use that interval. Peer replies remain unauthenticated estimates and
never alter TLS anchor policy.
.Pp
The command requires an authenticated SSH or enrolled local shell with its
operator control channel, and runs alone, without a pipeline or redirection.
There is no ambient clock-write authority in ordinary applications. For
read-only pipelines use
.Nm date Fl -status .
All corrections are boot-local; they do not persist to the RTC or through
reboot. No time-zone, NTS or clock-slew service is provided.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 on successful owner operation or accepted scheduling; 1 on refusal;
2 on invalid shell usage. Network success is reported separately in status.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr date 1 ,
.Xr time.conf 5 ,
.Xr absd-sh 1
